<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna Walentynowicz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna Walentynowicz was a Polish trade unionist and co-founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, the first recognised independent trade union in the Eastern Bloc. She worked at the Lenin Shipyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> from 1950, first as a welder and later as a crane operator. Her firing in August 1980 ignited the strike at the shipyard, which set off a wave of strikes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; the Interfactory Strike Committee based there became Solidarity, and by September more than one million workers were on strike. Called by some the mother of independent Poland, she was awarded the Order of the White Eagle in 2006 and was killed in the 2010 Polish Air Force Tu-154 crash near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-walentynowicz.jpg" alt="Anna Walentynowicz" width="330" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gosiak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anna_Walentynowicz_with_Paula_Dobriansky_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Poland in 1980 the crane operator <b>Anna Walentynowicz</b>, whose dismissal set off the Gdańsk strike, and the electrician Lech Wałęsa stood at the head of the movement that became Solidarity.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The strike of August 1980 started in defence of <b>Anna Walentynowicz</b> and over everyday economic issues, then became a solidarity strike in sympathy with strikes elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sadove, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.71,26.64&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Sadove on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5157 404 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anna Walentynowicz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5607 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5607 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.71,26.64&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
